class:: HarmonicStrideOsc
summary:: Geometrically weighted harmonic or inharmonic series
categories:: UGens>Generators>Deterministic
related:: Guides/NovelOscillators

description::

HarmonicStrideOsc creates a finite series from one signed phase accumulator.
code::stride:: sets the ratio spacing and code::decay:: geometrically weights
successive components. It returns sine and cosine versions of the same
spectrum.

Internally, the UGen evaluates a stable finite complex geometric sum rather
than running one oscillator per partial. Components at Nyquist are removed and
the final active component is smoothly faded. Changing code::stride:: changes
how the shared phase is read, producing spectral phase warping rather than
independently integrated partial phases.

The closest vanilla UGen is link::Classes/Blip::. Both generate a compact
harmonic spectrum, but HarmonicStrideOsc supports fractional spacing,
geometric amplitude decay, signed frequency, and quadrature outputs.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: freq
Signed base frequency in hertz. Negative values reverse phase motion.

argument:: stride
Positive ratio spacing between components. code::1:: gives the conventional
harmonic sequence; fractional values create inharmonic spacing.

argument:: decay
Geometric amplitude multiplier from just above code::-1:: to just below
code::1::. Negative values alternate component polarity.

argument:: phase
Readout phase offset in radians. It does not alter accumulated phase.

argument:: normalize
Crossfade from the raw sum at code::0:: to absolute-amplitude normalization at
code::1::.

argument:: maxPartials
Initialization-rate integer from code::1:: through code::16384::. It caps the
series before Nyquist culling.

argument:: mul
Multiplier applied to both outputs.

argument:: add
Offset added to both outputs after multiplication.

returns::
An array containing the sine-like sum followed by its cosine-like quadrature
sum.

method:: kr

HarmonicStrideOsc is audio-rate only. This method throws an error.

subsection:: Discussion

The output is bounded using the active absolute-amplitude sum when
code::normalize:: is enabled. A near-unity decay uses a stable limiting form,
so legal extreme values remain finite. At zero frequency, the phase stops and
the current complex sum is held.

examples::

code::
(
{ HarmonicStrideOsc.ar(110, 1, 0.8)[0] * 0.08 }.play;
)
::

code::
(
{
    var stride = LFNoise1.kr(0.3).range(0.4, 2.4);
    var decay = LFNoise1.kr(0.2).range(0.6, 0.96);
    Splay.ar(HarmonicStrideOsc.ar(55, stride, decay)) * 0.045;
}.play;
)
::

